dashboard.getRange("A33:H35").merge();dashboard.getRange("A33").values=[[`Yes—Project History intentionally keeps multiple observations when the same project appears in more than one source report period. A new row does not, by itself, mean the scope changed: it may simply be a recurring quarterly, annual, or utility TPR report. A crosswalk is the documented bridge between names/IDs in different source periods that represent the same project. For the conservative CAISO crosswalk links, filter Crosswalk ID, sort Observation date oldest to newest, and read Crosswalk evidence. A row can carry two link IDs when it bridges adjacent plan pairs. For the 2009 proposal snapshot, ${proposalCrosswalkReview.linked} of ${proposalCrosswalkReview.reviewed_proposals} rows (Highwind) is linked to a later final-plan record; ${proposalCrosswalkReview.unresolved} remain blank because similar geography alone is not identity evidence. Empty Crosswalk ID means no reviewed link, not a finding that the projects differ. To investigate a revision, compare Path or scope detail, reported cost, dates, and Change signal. The source locator and URL identify the exact underlying report row.`]];dashboard.getRange("A33").format={fill:C.lavender,font:{color:C.text,size:10},wrapText:true,verticalAlignment:"center",borders:{preset:"outside",style:"thin",color:C.line}};

